    "case_id": "771",
    "case_summary": "A 52-year-old woman with normal BMI (23.5), with a history of controlled asthma (no medication needed) and routine use of rosuvastatin (20 mg) daily. In October 2020, she experienced moderate severity symptoms including fever, weakness, headache, fatigue, and dry cough lasting for three weeks, not requiring hospitalization. A few months later and shortly after vaccination, she developed joint symptoms, followed by acute back pain and paresthesia. She also experienced morning stiffness and knee pain, more severe in the mornings and improving throughout the day. The symptoms eventually concentrated around the elbow.\n\n- Small red node and mild joint deformity at distal interphalangeal joint of left index finger- Multiple joint involvement affecting fingers, knee, and elbow- Back pain with associated paresthesia at the same location- Morning stiffness affecting multiple joints\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['- Total leucocyte count: 6.62 cells/μl (normal range)', '- C-reactive protein (CRP): normal', '- Rheumatoid factor (RF): negative', '- Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies: normal range', '- Anti-PR3 (C.A.N.C.A): normal range', '- P.A.N.C.A: normal range', '- SARS-CoV-2 PCR: initially positive, negative after 3 weeks', '- SARS-CoV-2 IgG: positive against Spike protein'], 'Imaging': {'Title': 'Figure 1', 'Description': 'Hebreden node at DIP of the left index finger of the case'}}",
